I’ll update the ruler so release positions snap to the nearest 10-unit tick, including the final resting position after a swipe.I’m going to inspect the current component and then patch the scroll-end behavior without changing the existing ruler styling.Implement the snap at scroll release by rounding the final horizontal offset to the nearest tick, then scrolling to that position:

```tsx
const snapToNearestTick = (offsetX: number) => {
  const index = Math.round(offsetX / tickSpacing);
  const clampedIndex = Math.max(0, Math.min(index, maxValue / 10));
  const snappedOffset = clampedIndex * tickSpacing;
  const snappedValue = clampedIndex * 10;

  rulerRef.current?.scrollTo({
    x: snappedOffset,
    animated: true,
  });

  onValueChange(snappedValue);
};
```

Attach it to the existing `ScrollView`:

```tsx
<ScrollView
  ref={rulerRef}
  horizontal
  snapToInterval={tickSpacing}
  onScroll={handleScroll}
  onScrollEndDrag={(event) => {
    snapToNearestTick(event.nativeEvent.contentOffset.x);
  }}
  onMomentumScrollEnd={(event) => {
    snapToNearestTick(event.nativeEvent.contentOffset.x);
  }}
>
```

This preserves the current styling and behavior while ensuring the ruler always settles on the nearest 10-unit tick after the user releases or finishes swiping.

